1
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:08,898
Penlock is an app for Passport Prime. This is the KeyOS simulator.

2
00:00:08,898 --> 00:00:13,557
Every launch opens with a warning about protecting your seed phrase.

3
00:00:13,557 --> 00:00:18,877
Back up a seed phrase on paper, or sign with cosigners. It all runs offline, and nothing is saved.

4
00:00:18,877 --> 00:00:21,794
Typos are flagged word by word. This is a public test phrase, never a real wallet.

5
00:00:21,794 --> 00:00:23,344
Type a 12 or 24-word phrase. Each word appears numbered, so you can check it.

6
00:00:23,344 --> 00:00:27,576
The BIP39 checksum is verified as you type.

7
00:00:27,576 --> 00:00:38,015
Penlock splits it into three paper shares. Any two rebuild the phrase. One alone reveals nothing.

8
00:00:38,015 --> 00:00:42,237
Copy each share onto its own strip, and keep the strips in different places.

9
00:00:42,237 --> 00:00:47,442
Restoring scans any two strips, on the device. Camera picture blanked for this recording.

10
00:00:47,442 --> 00:00:52,695
Handwritten phrases are read on the device too. You always check them against the paper.

11
00:00:52,695 --> 00:00:56,394
Now the new part: two shares can act as two signers.

12
00:00:56,394 --> 00:00:57,137
In the simulator the share is pasted. A Passport scans its strip instead.

13
00:00:57,137 --> 00:01:02,610
Each share derives its own signing key. Neither device sees the other share, or the phrase.

14
00:01:02,610 --> 00:01:07,263
The Passport shows its public key as a QR code. It can't spend.

15
00:01:07,263 --> 00:01:12,476
A second signer joins. Here that is a laptop tool holding share 2, standing in for a phone.

16
00:01:12,476 --> 00:01:18,777
Both devices derive the same shared address, without sending each other anything secret.

17
00:01:18,777 --> 00:01:25,434
The Passport shows exactly what you are approving: destination, change and fee.

18
00:01:25,434 --> 00:01:30,376
Round 1: each signer adds a one-time nonce. On a Passport this moves by QR code.

19
00:01:30,376 --> 00:01:34,495
Round 2: each signer adds a partial signature.

20
00:01:34,495 --> 00:01:38,599
The signatures join into one. On chain it looks like an ordinary single signature.

21
00:01:38,599 --> 00:01:42,745
Bitcoin Core accepted and confirmed this exact transaction on a private test network.

